Is Circle difficult in JEE Advanced compared to JEE Main?

Yes. JEE Advanced Circle questions involve multi-step reasoning - for example, combining locus derivation with a paragraph-format sub-question. JEE Main typically tests direct formula application. The shift from formula recall to logical construction is where most students lose marks, which is why practising PYQs with full solutions matters.

Which sub-topics of Circle are most important for JEE Advanced?

Tangents from external points, chord of contact, family of circles, orthogonal circles, and locus of midpoints are the most frequently tested. Questions on common tangents and the radical axis appear less often but carry high marks when they do appear. Focus on these five areas first

How many questions from Circle appear in JEE Advanced each year?

Typically 1-3 questions per year, contributing 3-9 marks. Between 2009 and 2018, Circle appeared in every single paper. The topic is most often tested alongside straight lines and conics in the same question or paragraph, so knowing all three together gives a compounding advantage.

Are NCERT examples enough preparation for Circle in JEE Advanced?

NCERT builds the foundation - definitions, standard results, basic tangent and chord problems - but it is not sufficient for JEE Advanced. You need to extend to pole-polar, radical axis, and locus derivations. Use Class 12 Maths NCERT Solutions to master NCERT, then move to JEE Advanced PYQs like the ones on this page

What is the chord of contact and why does it appear so often?

The chord of contact is the line joining the two points where tangents from an external point touch a circle. Its equation is T = 0. It appears frequently because it connects the idea of tangency, locus, and pole-polar relationships - all of which JEE Advanced examiners like to combine in a single question

How should I use these previous year questions in my study plan?

Solve each question unseen first, note whether you got stuck on the method or the algebra, then study the solution. Group your errors by type (wrong approach vs. calculation error). Spend one revision session per week re-solving questions you got wrong. eSaral's 5-layer doubt solving system helps you get personalised feedback on exactly these stuck points Leave a comment
